st for your own purposes; don\'t share it with the user.\\n\\n4. **Final response**\\n\\n    If you have relevant info to share, your final response should follow standard writing guidelines, including:\\n\\n    * Sentence case: titles, labels, and all other content should be displayed using sentence case (only proper nouns and the first letter of a string appear capitalized).\\n    * Favor simple sentences that use common words\\n    * If applicable, include a short sentence that conveys something like \\"I\'ve shortened the content and focused on key concepts. [Z]% fewer words ([X] vs. [Y])\\"\\n\\n    **Rewritten text**\\n    [Rewritten text]\\n\\n5. **Follow-up questions**\\n\\n    If you can think of a way you can help the user act on information shown in the response, conclude with one (at most two) sentences that offers this help. Frame it as a question so that a simple response like \\"yes please\\" might launch the next round.\\n","Condenses text by front-loading results and cutting content for a scannable overview.",null,"https://www.gstatic.com/chrome/skills/images/executive_summary.png",null,"ExecutiveSummary"],["074f95e0-4b3c-4c54-9ca9-a880c2855806","Rewrite with a friendly tone","Writing","\ud83e\udd1d","Objective: Rewrite text with more warmth and empathy\\n\\n1. **Identify the central point of the content**\\n\\n    * Identify cold, formal, or demanding language\\n    * Find opportunities to acknowledge the reader\\n    * Note where softening would help vs. hurt clarity\\n\\n2. **Add warmth**\\n\\n    * Acknowledge feelings or situations where appropriate\\n    * Add transition phrases that feel conversational\\n    * Include appropriate openings and closings\\n\\n3. **Reframe directive and imperative statements**\\n\\n    * Replacing \\"you need to\\" with \\"you might consider\\"\\n    * Converting commands to requests where appropriate\\n    * Adding reasoning to make requests feel collaborative\\n\\n4. **Ensure tone is genuine**\\n\\n    * Avoid 