unt:** [Y] ([Z]% reduction)\\n    **Shortened:** [Compressed text]\\n    **Original:** [X] words\\n    **What was cut**\\n    * [Category of cuts made]\\n\\n7. **Follow-up questions**\\n\\n    If you can think of a way you can help the user act on information shown in the response, conclude with one (at most two) sentences that offers this help. Frame it as a question so that a simple response like \\"yes please\\" might launch the next round.\\n","Cuts word count by half, removing filler and redundancy while keeping the meaning.",null,"https://www.gstatic.com/chrome/skills/images/shorten_text.png",null,"ShortenText"],["2d1ff227-4210-4c07-8a86-9d411031f478","Draft a professional email","Writing","\ud83d\udce7","Objective: Convert content to a professional email format\\n\\n1. **Identify the central point of the content**\\n\\n    * Find the core idea or main argument\\n    * Identify what the author wants readers to believe or do\\n    * Reflect on the \\"why?\\" of the content\\n    * Note the scope and limitations of the content\\n    * Determine the intended recipient, if possible\\n    * Determine a sense of urgency, if any\\n\\n2. **Subject line creation**: Write a clear, specific subject:\\n\\n    * Under 50 characters when possible\\n    * Include action verb if requesting something\\n    * Be specific enough to stand out in an inbox\\n\\n3. **Opening construction**\\n\\n    * Appropriate greeting for the context; one sentence\\n    * Get to the point of the email in the second sentence. Consider making this sentence a self-standing paragraph\\n\\n4. **Structure the rest of the email for scanability**\\n\\n    * One idea per paragraph\\n    * Short paragraphs (2-4 sentences max)\\n    * Make action items explicit and easy to find\\n    * Favor lists and bold labels if it improves scanability\\n\\n5. **End appropriately**\\n\\n    * Match formality to the relationship\\n    * Include next steps if applicable\\n    * Professional sign-off\\n\\n6. **Handling exceptions**\\n\\n    Prioritize excellent content in your response. If you\'re unable to formulate a response that meets all criteria, you should\\n    * respond as best you can and\\n    * acknowledge any limitations or challenges you faced. For example, maybe there wasn\'t sufficient content on a webpage or the content wasn\'t compatible with a given request.\\n\\n    Consider your proposed response objectively and rate it on a scale from 1-10. If you wouldn\'t give it a 10, either try to create a stronger response or consider acknowledging any limitations or challenges you faced. The score is just for your own purposes; don\'t share it with the user.\\n    * No selection \u2192 Ask user to highlight source text\\n\\n7. **Final response**\\n\\n    If you have relevant info to share, your final response should follow standard writing guidelines, including:\\n\\n    * Sentence case: titles, labels, and all other content should be displayed using sentence case (only proper nouns and the first letter of a string appear capitalized).\\n    * Favor simple sentences that use common words\\n    **Draft email**\\n    Include a horizontal line to separate draft content\\n\\n    **Subject:** [Clear, specific subject line]\\n\\n    [Greeting],\\n\\n    [Body paragraphs]\\n\\n    [Sign-off],\\n    [Name]\\n\\n    Include a horizontal line to separate the draft content from explanatory text.\\n\\n    If you think it\'s useful, with one sentence include any assumptions you made that you think might surprise the user or affect them to modify the draft content.\\n    [Any assumptions made about context]\\n\\n8. **Follow-up questions**\\n\\n    If you can think of a way you can help the user act on information shown in the response, conclude with one (at most two) sentences that offers this help. Frame it as a question so that a simple response like \\"yes please\\" might launch the next round.\\n","Converts content into a structured email with a subject line and action items.",null,"https://www.gstatic.com/chrome/skills/images/draft_a_professional_email.png",null,"DraftAProfessionalEmail"],["85336e0c-3047-4bd1-9a91-5c5ef91e9537","Suggest replies","Writing","\ud83d\udea6","Objective: Create 3 possible replies\\n\\n1. **Identify the central point of the content**\\n\\n    * Find the core idea or main argument\\n    * Identify what the author wants readers to believe or do\\n    * Reflect on the \\"why?\\" of the content\\n    * Note the scope and limitations of the content\\n    * Note the tone (formal, casual, urgent, sensitive)\\n    * Determine what kind of response is expected\\n\\n2. **Option 1: Craft a quick response**\\n\\n    * Answers the core question directly\\n    * Keeps to 2-3 sentences\\n    * Works when you\'re busy or the answer is simple\\n\\n3. **Option 2: Complete response**\\n\\n    * Addresses all points raised\\n    * Provides context and detail\\n    * Works when thoroughness matters\\n\\n4. **Option 3: Detailed careful response**\\n\\n    * Uses measured, thoughtful phrasing\\n    * Acknowledges complexity or sensitivity\\n    * Works when tone really matters or the situation is delicate\\n\\n5. **Handling exceptions**\\n\\n    Prioritize excellent content in your response. If you\'re unable to formulate a response that meets all criteria, you should\\n    * respond as best you can and\\n    * acknowledge any limitations or challenges you faced. For example, maybe there wasn\'t sufficient content on a webpage or the content wasn\'t compatible with a given request.\\n\\n    Consider your proposed response objectively and rate it on a scale from 1-10. If you wouldn\'t give it a 10, either try to create a stronger response or consider acknowledging any limitations or challenges you faced. The score is just for your own purposes; don\'t share it with the user.\\n    * Context unclear \u2192 Provide options ranging brief to detailed\\n    * No message visible \u2192 Ask user to navigate to the message\\n\\n6. **Final response**\\n\\n    If you have relevant info to share, your final response should follow standard writing guidelines, including:\\n\\n    * Sentence case: titles, labels, and all other content should be displayed using sentence case (only proper nouns and the first letter of a string appear capitalized).\\n    * Favor simple sentences that use common words\\n\\n    **In short:** [What you\'re replying to]\\n\\n    **Option 1 \u2014 Brief:** [Quick, to-the-point reply]\\n\\n    **Option 2 \u2014 Thorough:** [Complete response addressing all points]\\n\\n    **Option 3 \u2014 Diplomatic:** [Careful phrasing for sensitive situations]\\n\\n7. **Follow-up questions**\\n\\n    If you can think of a way you can help the user act on information shown in the response, conclude with one (at most two) sentences that offers this help. Frame it as a question so that a simple response like \\"yes please\\" might launch the next round.\\n","Analyzes tone to offer three replies: a quick note, full answer, and diplomatic response.",null,"https://www.gstatic.com/chrome/skills/images/suggest_replies.png",null,"SuggestReplies"],["8f1d4e14-0e94-415b-b0c1-51020c319f7b","Translate text","Writing","\ud83c\udf10","Objective: Translate to specified language\\n\\n1. **Identify languages**\\n\\n    * Determine the source language\\n    * Confirm the target language (if not specified, use the user agent\'s configured language)\\n    * Note any dialect considerations\\n\\n2. **Consider context**\\n\\n    * Preserve the original meaning and tone\\n    * Adapt idioms for meaning, not literal translation\\n    * Maintain formatting and structure\\n    * Identify cultural references that may not translate\\n    * Offer alternative phrasings for ambiguous terms\\n\\n3. **Provide transparency**\\n\\n    * Note any particularly difficult translations\\n    * Offer alternatives for key terms if multiple exist\\n    * Identify any meaning that may be lost in translation\\n\\n4. **Handling exceptions**\\n\\n    