lateText"],["dfe2bafa-d66b-4642-a293-d3a3b71f7afd","Explain slang or jargon","Writing","\ud83e\udde2","Objective: Translate slang or jargon to common language\\n\\n1. **Identify a term or terms to focus on**\\n\\n    * Has the user highlighted a term?\\n    * Determine the context\\n    * If the user hasn\'t indicated a  term, choose a term from the site the user is currently viewing. Do not choose a term yourself.\\n\\n2. **Professional translation**\\n\\n    * How to express the same sentiment professionally\\n    * Formal alternatives that preserve the meaning\\n    * Note any variations in meaning\\n\\n3. **Define in plain language**\\n\\n    * What the term actually communicates\\n    * The emotional or social connotation\\n    * Who typically uses this term\\n\\n4. **Explain a term\'s background**\\n\\n    * Where the term comes from (platform, community, event)\\n    * How it evolved or spread\\n    * Why it resonates with its users\\n\\n5. **Advise on appropriate use**\\n\\n    * When using the original is fine\\n    * When to definitely use the professional version\\n    * Risk of using it incorrectly\\n\\n6. **Handling exceptions**\\n\\n    Prioritize excellent content in your response. If you\'re unable to formulate a response that meets all criteria, you should\\n    * respond as best you can and\\n    * acknowledge any limitations or challenges you faced. For example, maybe there wasn\'t sufficient content on a webpage or the content wasn\'t compatible with a given request.\\n\\n    Consider your proposed response objectively and rate it on a scale from 1-10. If you wouldn\'t give it a 10, either try to create a stronger response or consider acknowledging any limitations or challenges you faced. The score is just for your own purposes; don\'t share it with the user.\\n\\n7. **Final response**\\n\\n    If you have relevant info to share, your final response should follow standard writing guidelines, including:\\n\\n    * Sentence case: titles, labels, and all other content should be displayed using sentence case (only proper nouns and the first letter of a string appear capitalized).\\n    * Favor simple sentences that use common words\\n\\n    **Term:** \\"[highlighted slang]\\"\\n\\n    **Professional translation:** [How to say this in a work/formal context]\\n\\n    **What it actually means:** [Plain-language explanation]\\n\\n    **Origin/context:** [Where this came from, who uses it]\\n\\n    **Usage notes:**\\n\\n    * When it\'s appropriate to use the original\\n    * When to use the professional version\\n    * Risk of misuse\\n\\n    **Example:**\\n\\n    * Slang: \\"[example sentence]\\"\\n    * Professional: \\"[same sentiment, formal language]\\"\\n\\n    **Follow-up questions**\\n\\n    If you can think of a way you can help the user act on information shown in the response, conclude with one (at most two) sentences that offers this help. Frame it as a question so that a simple response like \\"yes please\\" might launch the next round.\\n","Decodes informal terms and their origins while advising on usage and alternatives.",null,"https://www.gstatic.com/chrome/skills/images/explain_slang_or_jargon.png",null,"ExplainSlangOrJargon"],["6fdc5186-9d49-4434-8305-9ff9357fd382","Check job fit","Research","\ud83e\udde4","Objective: Compare a job description with someone\'s profile\\n\\n1. **Identify the central point of the content**\\n\\n    * Identify required skills and qualifications\\n    * Identify preferred or bonus qualifications\\n    * Identify key responsibilities and scope\\n    * Identify experience level and years required\\n\\n2. **Compare against candidate profile or resume**\\n\\n    * User provides resume/profile OR skill uses stored context\\n    * Match stated skills to required skills\\n    * Identify relevant experience for each responsibility\\n\\n3. **Calculate alignment**\\n\\n    * Score 0-10 based on requirement coverage\\n    * Weight required qualifications more heavily than preferred\\n    * Account for adjacent/transferable skills\\n\\n4. **Identify areas of mismatch**\\n\\n    * List requirements not met\\n    * Categorize gaps as skill gaps, experience gaps, or credential gaps\\n    * Suggest how to address each gap in an interview\\n\\n5. **Make a recommendation**\\n\\n    * Based on all preceding steps, form a recommendation\\n    * The objective is to give the user a gut check\\n    * Yes / Maybe / Probably not with reasoning\\n\\n6. **Handling exceptions**\\n\\n    Prioritize excellent content in your response. If you\'re unable to formulate a response that meets all criteria, you should\\n    * respond as best you can and\\n    * acknowledge any limitations or challenges you faced. For example, maybe there wasn\'t sufficient content on a webpage or the content wasn\'t compatible with a given request.\\n\\n    Consider your proposed response objectively and rate it on a scale from 1-10. If you wouldn\'t give it a 10, either try to create a stronger response or consider acknowledging any limitations or challenges you faced. The score is just for your own purposes; don\'t share it with the user.\\n\\n7. **Final response**\\n\\n    If you have relevant info to share, your final response should follow standard writing guidelines, including:\\n\\n    * Sentence case: titles, labels, and all other content should be displayed using sentence case (only proper nouns and the first letter of a string appear capitalized).\\n    * Favor simple sentences that use common words\\n\\n    **Fit score:** [X]/10\\n    [One sentence explaining the score]\\n\\n    **Strong matches**\\n\\n    | Requirement | Your qualification | Match strength |\\n    | :---: | :---: | :---: |\\n\\n    **Possible gaps**\\n\\n    | Requirement | Gap | How to Address in Interview |\\n    | :---: | :---: | :---: |\\n\\n8. **Follow-up questions**\\n\\n    If you can think of a way you can help the user act on information shown in the response, conclude with one (at most two) sentences that offers this help. Frame it as a question so that a simple response like \\"yes please\\" might launch the next round.\\n    * Specific ways to help: talking points for an interview, keywords the candidate might use in an interview, how to positively frame gaps\\n","Compares a candidate to job requirements to find gaps and an alignment score.",null,"https://www.gstatic.com/chrome/skills/images/check_job_fit.png",null,"CheckJobFit"],["c8a2ca3c-d1a9-4811-bb2a-496e7002f13d","Prep for today","Research","\ud83c\udf05","Objective: Create a morning briefing based on today\'s calendar\\n\\n1. **Scan calendar for today\'s schedule**\\n\\n    * All events with times and durations\\n    * Meeting titles and attendees\\n    * Location or video links\\n    * If on calendar.google.com, avoid linking to individual events because it won\'t work\\n\\n2. **Identify preparation requirements**\\n\\n    * Meetings requiring pre-reads or materials\\n    * Conversations needing talking points\\n    * Decisions to be made\\n\\n3. **Find gaps for focused work**\\n\\n    * Gaps between meetings over 60 minutes\\n    * Morning vs. afternoon availability\\n    * Back-to-back stretches to flag\\n\\n4. **Note logistical concerns**\\n\\n    * Tight transitions between meetings\\n    * Location changes requiring travel\\n    * Unusual scheduling (early/late meetings)\\n\\n5. **Order tasks**\\n\\n    * Most important meeting preparation first\\n    * Time-sensitive items flagged\\n    * Quick wins vs. substantial prep\\n\\n6. **Handling exceptions**\\n\\n    Prioritize excellent content in your response. If you\'re unable to formulate a response that meets all criteria, you should\\n    * respond as best you can and\\n    * acknowledge any limitations or challenges you faced. For example, maybe there wasn\'t sufficient content on a webpage or the content wasn\'t compatible with a given request.\\n\\n    Consider your proposed response objectively and rate it on a scale from 1-10. If you 