    If you have relevant info to share, your final response should follow standard writing guidelines, including:\\n\\n    * Sentence case: titles, labels, and all other content should be displayed using sentence case (only proper nouns and the first letter of a string appear capitalized).\\n    * Favor simple sentences that use common words\\n\\n    **In short:** [your recommendation, if applicable. Assess value: typical pricing, bulk discounts, brand premium?]\\n    **Price:** [total]\\n    **Quantity:** [size/count]\\n\\n    **Unit price:**\\n\\n    * Per oz/ml: $X\\n    * Per serving: $X\\n    * Per use (estimated): $X\\n\\n    **Hidden costs:**\\n\\n    * [Shipping, subscription, consumables, etc.]\\n\\n9. **Follow-up questions**\\n\\n    If you can think of a way you can help the user act on information shown in the response, conclude with one (at most two) sentences that offers this help. Frame it as a question so that a simple response like \\"yes please\\" might launch the next round.\\n","Factors in total costs, including shipping fees, to calculate a true cost.",null,"https://www.gstatic.com/chrome/skills/images/price_per_unit.png",null,"PricePerUnit"],["41a6530f-5a32-48b2-81fb-45abfd767b44","Check returns","Shopping","\u21a9\ufe0f","Objective: Get help returning a purchase\\n\\n1. **Find return window**\\n\\n    * Number of days allowed\\n    * Start date (from purchase, delivery, etc.)\\n    * Holiday extensions if mentioned\\n\\n2. **Identify return conditions**\\n\\n    * New/unused required?\\n    * Original packaging needed?\\n    * Tags attached?\\n    * Receipt required?\\n\\n3. **Understand how to return**\\n\\n    * Return shipping: who pays?\\n    * In-store vs. mail options\\n    * Refund method (original payment, store credit)\\n    * Processing time\\n\\n4. **Find items that can\'t be returned**\\n\\n    * Final sale categories\\n    * Hygiene-restricted items\\n    * Personalized/custom items\\n    * Clearance restrictions\\n\\n5. **Identify unusual or hidden terms**\\n\\n    * Restocking fees\\n    * Condition for refund vs. exchange\\n    * Time limits for different product types\\n\\n6. **Handling exceptions**\\n\\n    Prioritize excellent content in your response. If you\'re unable to formulate a response that meets all criteria, you should\\n    * respond as best you can and\\n    * acknowledge any limitations or challenges you faced. For example, maybe there wasn\'t sufficient content on a webpage or the content wasn\'t compatible with a given request.\\n\\n    Consider your proposed response objectively and rate it on a scale from 1-10. If you wouldn\'t give it a 10, either try to create a stronger response or consider acknowledging any limitations or challenges you faced. The score is just for your own purposes; don\'t share it with the user.\\n\\n7. **Final response**\\n\\n    If you have relevant info to share, your final response should follow standard writing guidelines, including:\\n\\n    * Sentence case: titles, labels, and all other content should be displayed using sentence case (only proper nouns and the first letter of a string appear capitalized).\\n    * Favor simple sentences that use common words\\n    * At the end of your initial response, inform the user: \\"Final costs may vary, always verify at checkout\\"\\n\\n    **In short:**\\n\\n    * Return window: [X] days\\n    * Condition required: [new/opened/any]\\n    * Refund method: [original payment/store credit]\\n    * Who pays shipping: [you/them]\\n\\n    **Exceptions:** [Items that can\'t be returned]\\n\\n    **Things to look out for:** [Unusual terms or gotchas]\\n\\n8. **Follow-up questions**\\n\\n    If you can think of a way you can help the user act on information shown in the response, conclude with one (at most two) sentences that offers this help. Frame it as a question so that a simple response like \\"yes please\\" might launch the next round.\\n","Gathers refund details like deadlines, restocking fees, and return-shipping costs.",null,"https://www.gstatic.com/chrome/skills/images/check_returns.png",null,"CheckReturns"],["c26a0992-39fd-45fa-ad33-7522dd673a19","Sizing help","Shopping","\ud83d\udc55","Objective: Get help picking the right size\\n\\n1. **Find official guidance**\\n\\n    * Published size chart\\n    * Measurement recommendations\\n    * Fit description (slim, regular, relaxed)\\n\\n2. **Find sizing patterns in reviews**\\n\\n    * \\"Runs small\\" / \\"Runs large\\" / \\"True to size\\" frequency\\n    * Specific sizing complaints\\n    * Recommendations from reviewers\\n\\n3. **Calculate consensus in reviews**\\n\\n    * Percentage of reviewers mentioning sizing issues\\n    * Direction of sizing issues\\n    * Confidence based on review volume\\n\\n4. **Note fit variations based on body types**\\n\\n    * Feedback from different body types\\n    * Length vs. width issues\\n    * Between-size guidance\\n\\n5. **Make a recommendation**\\n\\n    * Based on all preceding steps, form a recommendation\\n    * The objective is to give the user a gut check\\n    * Order usual size, size up, or size down\\n    * Check specific measurements if possible\\n    * Return policy consideration for sizing\\n\\n6. **Handling exceptions**\\n\\n    Prioritize excellent content in your response. If you\'re unable to formulate a response that meets all criteria, you should\\n    * respond as best you can and\\n    * acknowledge any limitations or challenges you faced. For example, maybe there wasn\'t sufficient content on a webpage or the content wasn\'t compatible with a given request.\\n\\n    Consider your proposed response objectively and rate it on a scale from 1-10. If you wouldn\'t give it a 10, either try to create a stronger response or consider acknowledging any limitations or challenges you faced. The score is just for your own purposes; don\'t share it with the user.\\n\\n7. **Final response**\\n\\n    If you have relevant info to share, your final response should follow standard writing guidelines, including:\\n\\n    * Sentence case: titles, labels, and all other content should be displayed using sentence case (only proper nouns and the first letter of a string appear capitalized).\\n    * Favor simple sentences that use common words\\n    **In short:**\\n    * [Order usual size / Size up / Size down]\\n    * [Specific advice]\\n\\n    **What reviews say:**\\n\\n    * Runs: [Small / True to size / Large]\\n    * Frequency of sizing complaints: [X]%\\n\\n    **Size chart:** [If visible, key measurements]\\n\\n    **Notes:** [Body type feedback, between-sizes advice]\\n\\n8. **Follow-up questions**\\n\\n    If you can think of a way you can help the user act on information shown in the response, conclude with one (at most two) sentences that offers this help. Frame it as a question so that a simple response like \\"yes please\\" might launch the next round.\\n","Uses customer feedback to determine if an item runs small, large, or true to size.",null,"https://www.gstatic.com/chrome/skills/images/sizing_help.png",null,"SizingHelp"],["d3912c37-8734-429c-9fbe-f2436bba0521","Check warranty","Shopping","\ud83d\udee1\ufe0f","Objective: Get help figuring out if a purchase is still covered by warranty\\n\\n1. **Find what\'s protected**\\n\\n    * Duration (years, lifetime, limited)\\n    * What\'s covered (defects, damage, wear)\\n    * Who provides it (manufacturer, seller, third party)\\n\\n2. **Find what\'s NOT covered**\\n\\n    * Wear and tear\\n    * Accidental damage\\n    * Commercial use\\n    * Unauthorized modifications\\n\\n3. **How to use the warranty**\\n\\n    * Registration required?\\n    * Proof of purchase needed?\\n    * Contact method\\n    * Typical processing time\\n\\n4. **Evaluate the warranty**\\n\\n    * Is this typical for the product category?\\n    * Better or worse than competitors?\\n    * Is the warranty a selling point?\\n\\n5. **Handling exceptions**\\n\\n    Prioritize excellent content in your response. If you\'re unable to formulate a response that meets all criteria, you should\\n    * respond as best you can and\\n    * acknowledge any limitations or challenges you faced. For example, maybe there wasn\'t sufficient content on a webpage or the content wasn\'t compatible with a given request.\\n\\n    Consider your proposed response objectively and rate it on a scale from 1-10. If you wouldn\'t give it a 10, either try to create a stronger response or consider acknowledging any limitations or challenges you faced. The score is just for your own purposes; don\'t share it with the user.\\n\\n6. **Final response**\\n\\n    If you have relevant info to share, your final response should follow standard writing guidelines, including:\\n\\n    * Sentence case: titles, labels, and all other content should be displayed using sentence case (only proper nouns and the first letter of a string appear capitalized).\\n    * Favor simple sentences that use common words\\n\\n    **Warranty**\\n\\n    * Duration: [X years / lifetime / limited]\\n    * What\'s covered: [defects, damage, etc.]\\n    * Provider: [manufacturer/seller/third party]\\n    **Exclusions:** [What\'s NOT covered]\\n    **Claim Process:** [How to claim, registration needed?]\\n    **Assessment:** [For this product type, is this typical/good/bad?]\\n\\n7. **Follow-up questions**\\n\\n    If you can think of a way you can help the user act on information shown in the response, conclude with one (at most two) sentences that offers this help. Frame it as a question so that a simple response like \\"yes please\\" might launch the next round.\\n","Gathers product-protection info like covered defects, exclusions, and the claim process.",null,"https://www.gstatic.com/chrome/skills/images/check_warranty.png",null,"CheckWarranty"],["19309e05-0f5b-4f5c-870a-9975a743f1e2","Rate as gift","Shopping","\ud83c\udf81","Objective: Get help deciding if a product makes a good gift\\n\\n1. **Assess price**\\n\\n    * What rela