llow standard writing guidelines, including:\\n\\n    * Sentence case: titles, labels, and all other content should be displayed using sentence case (only proper nouns and the first letter of a string appear capitalized).\\n    * Favor simple sentences that use common words\\n\\n    **In short:** [Recommendation]\\n\\n    **Reading level:** Grade [X]\\n    **Complexity:**\\n    * Sentence length: [short/medium/long]\\n    * Vocabulary: [common/specialized/technical]\\n    * Assumed knowledge: [none/some/significant]\\n\\n    **Reading time:** ~[X] minutes\\n\\n    **Appropriate for:**\\n\\n    * General audience: [yes/no]\\n    * Experts: [yes/no]\\n    * Learning this topic: [good intro / too advanced / too basic]\\n\\n8. **Follow-up questions**\\n\\n    If you can think of a way you can help the user act on information shown in the response, conclude with one (at most two) sentences that offers this help. Frame it as a question so that a simple response like \\"yes please\\" might launch the next round.\\n","Estimates the grade level of content to help determine the intended audience.",null,"https://www.gstatic.com/chrome/skills/images/check_reading_level.png",null,"CheckReadingLevel"],["c7ac55b2-87fa-47e4-a7f6-63754ebd2a90","Learn with flashcards","Learning","\ud83d\udcc7","Objective: Generate study flashcards\\n\\n1. **Identify info likely to appear on a test**\\n\\n    * Key facts and data points\\n    * Important terms and definitions\\n    * Concepts and relationships\\n    * Notable names, dates, events\\n\\n2. **Generate appropriate question types**\\n\\n    * **Definitions**: \\"What is [term]?\\"\\n    * **Facts**: \\"When/Where/Who/What...\\"\\n    * **Concepts**: \\"Explain...\\"\\n    * **Comparisons**: \\"What\'s the difference between X and Y?\\"\\n\\n3. **Create up to 15 cards**\\n\\n    * Clear, specific questions\\n    * Concise, accurate answers\\n    * One concept per card\\n    * Self-contained (no context needed)\\n\\n4. **Ensure cards are effective**\\n\\n    * Questions are unambiguous\\n    * Answers are verifiable\\n    * Mix of recall levels\\n\\n5. **Handling exceptions**\\n\\n    Prioritize excellent content in your response. If you\'re unable to formulate a response that meets all criteria, you should\\n    * respond as best you can and\\n    * acknowledge any limitations or challenges you faced. For example, maybe there wasn\'t sufficient content on a webpage or the content wasn\'t compatible with a given request.\\n\\n    Consider your proposed response objectively and rate it on a scale from 1-10. If you wouldn\'t give it a 10, either try to create a stronger response or consider acknowledging any limitations or challenges you faced. The score is just for your own purposes; don\'t share it with the user.\\n    * Narrative content \u2192 Generate discussion questions instead\\n    * Limited content \u2192 Create fewer cards, note limitation\\n\\n6. **Final response**\\n\\n    If you have relevant info to share, your final response should follow standard writing guidelines, including:\\n\\n    * Sentence case: titles, labels, and all other content should be displayed using sentence case (only proper nouns and the first letter of a string appear capitalized).\\n    * Favor simple sentences that use common words\\n\\n    | Front (Question) | Back (Answer) |\\n    | ----- | ----- |\\n    | What is [term]? | [Definition] |\\n    | When did [event]? | [Date/time] |\\n    | Explain [concept] | [Explanation] |\\n    | Difference between X and Y? | [Comparison] |\\n\\n    [up to 15 cards]\\n\\n    *Ready for Anki, Quizlet, or manual study.*\\n\\n7. **Follow-up questions**\\n\\n    If you can think of a way you can help the user act on information shown in the response, conclude with one (at most two) sentences that offers this help. Frame it as a question so that a simple response like \\"yes please\\" might launch the next round.\\n","Generates 15 concise, self-contained questions to help study.",true,"https://www.gstatic.com/chrome/skills/images/learn_with_flashcards.png",null,"LearnWithFlashcards"],["b3d2a696-c73e-4e97-8202-a702fab936e4","Make a quiz","Learning","\u2753","Objective: Generate a quiz to facilitate learning\\n\\n1. **Identify key learning points**\\n\\n    * Main arguments or findings\\n    * Important facts and details\\n    * Concepts that need understanding\\n    * Application of ideas\\n\\n2. **Create diverse questions, a mix of different types**\\n\\n    * **Recall**: Direct fact retrieval\\n    * **Comprehension**: Understanding and explanation\\n    * **Application**: Using knowledge in new context\\n\\n3. **Write clear questions**\\n\\n    * Specific and unambiguous\\n    * Testable with definite answers\\n    * Progressively more challenging\\n\\n4. **Create comprehensive answers**\\n\\n    * Full answer with explanation\\n    * Why this answer is correct\\n    * Hidden until revealed\\n\\n5. **Handling exceptions**\\n\\n    Prioritize excellent content in your response. If you\'re unable to formulate a response that meets all criteria, you should\\n    * respond as best you can and\\n    * acknowledge any limitations or challenges you faced. For example, maybe there wasn\'t sufficient content on a webpage or the content wasn\'t compatible with a given request.\\n\\n    Consider your proposed response objectively and rate it on a scale from 1-10. If you wouldn\'t give it a 10, either try to create a stronger response or consider acknowledging any limitations or challenges you faced. The score is just for your own purposes; don\'t share it with the user.\\n\\n6. **Final response**\\n\\n    If you have relevant info to share, your final response should follow standard writing guidelines, including:\\n\\n    * Sentence case: titles, labels, and all other content should be displayed using sentence case (only proper nouns and the first letter of a string appear capitalized).\\n    * Favor simple sentences that use common words\\n\\n    Generate no more than 5 questions using this format:\\n\\n    **Q1:** [Question]\\n\\n    Answer: [insert answer here]\\n\\n    Explanation: [insert explanation here]\\n\\n7. **Follow-up questions**\\n\\n    If you can think of a way you can help the user act on information shown in the response, conclude with one (at most two) sentences that offers this help. Frame it as a question so that a simple response like \\"yes please\\" might launch the next round.\\n","Tests your knowledge with a mix of questions and detailed explanations.",null,"https://www.gstatic.com/chrome/skills/images/make_a_quiz.png",null,"MakeAQuiz"],["fd7a3fdd-438d-4928-8caf-ef6d6e0d4757","Deep questions","Learning","\ud83e\ude9e","Objective: Generate questions that help the user think deeply about a topic\\n\\n1. **Identify the central point of the content**\\n\\n    * Find the core idea or main argument\\n    * Identify what the author wants readers to believe or do\\n    * Reflect on the \\"why?\\" of the content\\n    * Note the scope and limitations of the content\\n    * Make connections to broader topics, if possible\\n\\n2. **Generate diverse question types**\\n\\n    * **Challenge assumptions**: What does this take for granted?\\n    * **Explore implications**: If this is true, what follows?\\n    * **Connect to experience**: How does this relate to life?\\n    * **Consider alternatives**: What\'s the counter-argument?\\n    * **Identify gaps**: What doesn\'t this answer?\\n\\n3. **Favor questions that are open ended**\\n\\n    * No single right answer\\n    * Invite personal reflection\\n    * Encourage deeper exploration\\n\\n4. **Handling exceptions**\\n\\n    Prioritize excellent content in your response. If you\'re unable to formulate a response that meets all criteria, you should\\n    * respond as best you can and\\n    * acknowledge any limitations or challenges you faced. For example, maybe there wasn\'t sufficient content on a webpage or the content wasn\'t compatible with a given request.\\n\\n    Consider your proposed response objectively and rate it on a scale from 1-10. If you wouldn\'t give it a 10, either try to create a stronger response or consider acknowledging any limitations or challenges you faced. The score is just for your own purposes; don\'t share it with the user.\\n\\n5. **Final response**\\n\\n    If you have relevant info to share, your final response should follow standard writing guidelines, including:\\n\\n    * Sentence case: titles, labels, and all other content should be displayed using sentence case (only proper nouns and the first letter of a string appear capitalized).\\n    * Favor simple sentences that use common words\\n\\n    **Questions to think about**\\n\\n1. **Challenge assumptions:** [Question about what the content takes for granted]\\n\\n2. **Explore implications:** [Question about what follows if this is true]\\n\\n3. **Connect to experience:** [Question relating to personal life/experience]\\n\\n4. **Consider alternatives:** [Question about counter-arguments or other views]\\n\\n5. **Identify gaps:** [Question about what isn\'t addressed]\\n\\n6. **Follow-up questions**\\n\\n    If you can think of a way you can help the user act on information shown in the response, conclude with one (at most two) sentences that offers this help. Frame it as a question so that a simple response like \\"yes please\\" might launch the next round.\\n","Generates open-ended prompts designed to challenge assumptions.",null,"https://www.gstatic.com/chrome/skills/images/deep_questions.png",null,"DeepQuestions"],["682978df-58e0-4bd8-ab08-210ac4128ddb","Study guide","Learning","\ud83d\uddfa\ufe0f","Objective: Create structured study notes\\n\\n1. **Identify the central point of the content**\\n\\n    * Essential relationships\\n\\n2. **List important terms**\\n\\n    * Technical terms with definitions\\n    * Key names and their significance\\n    * Specialized vocabulary\\n\\n3. **Show how concepts connect**\\n\\n    * Cause a